WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.433 --> 00:00:06.953
On this map, each pond's frogs last only about a decade.

2
00:00:08.293 --> 00:00:11.293
So why can the region still have frogs a century later?

3
00:00:14.367 --> 00:00:19.127
Each pond holds a local population, the frogs that live and breed there.

4
00:00:20.367 --> 00:00:22.327
The fields between are hostile ground.

5
00:00:23.747 --> 00:00:27.287
Ponds linked by dispersal like this form a metapopulation.

6
00:00:28.367 --> 00:00:31.027
Richard Levins asked the question simply.

7
00:00:32.407 --> 00:00:34.147
Forget how many frogs a pond holds.

8
00:00:35.487 --> 00:00:37.727
Each pond is either occupied or empty.

9
00:00:39.027 --> 00:00:42.087
Then follow one number: the fraction of ponds occupied.

10
00:00:43.667 --> 00:00:44.967
Now let the map run.

11
00:00:46.347 --> 00:00:48.847
Ponds go grey as their populations die out.

12
00:00:50.227 --> 00:00:52.007
Others turn teal as colonists arrive.

13
00:00:53.347 --> 00:00:57.707
Which ponds are teal keeps changing, yet the teal fraction hovers near three

14
00:00:57.707 --> 00:00:58.707
quarters.

15
00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:01.670
Losses are the simple side of the ledger.

16
00:01:02.990 --> 00:01:07.450
Each occupied pond loses its population at a steady extinction rate.

17
00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:12.470
So total losses grow in a straight line with the fraction occupied.

18
00:01:13.633 --> 00:01:14.993
Gains are different.

19
00:01:16.513 --> 00:01:21.253
Colonists must come from an occupied pond, and they can only found a population in

20
00:01:21.253 --> 00:01:24.973
an empty one. So gains need both kinds of pond.

21
00:01:26.067 --> 00:01:28.827
With no ponds occupied, there are no colonists.

22
00:01:30.247 --> 00:01:33.187
With every pond occupied, there is nowhere to land.

23
00:01:34.687 --> 00:01:37.727
Between those two ends, gains rise and then fall in a hump.

24
00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:42.380
Where the gold hump meets the red line, gains equal losses.

25
00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:45.720
The occupied fraction settles there.

26
00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:52.820
That level is one minus the ratio of extinction to colonization, With these rates,

27
00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:54.120
it sits at three quarters.

28
00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:58.960
Now halve the colonization rate, and keep extinction the same.

29
00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:02.140
Does the occupied fraction halve too?

30
00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:05.140
Make your guess before the graph moves.

31
00:02:11.633 --> 00:02:17.873
No. The hump shrinks, and the crossing slides only from three quarters to one half.

32
00:02:19.233 --> 00:02:22.973
What matters is the ratio of the two rates, not either one alone.

33
00:02:24.083 --> 00:02:25.903
Now push extinction up instead.

34
00:02:27.243 --> 00:02:30.083
The red line steepens, and the crossing slides left.

35
00:02:31.423 --> 00:02:35.843
When extinction equals colonization, the line only touches the hump at zero.

36
00:02:37.243 --> 00:02:39.083
No crossing above zero is left.

37
00:02:40.083 --> 00:02:45.443
So, in the model, a region keeps a species only while colonization beats extinction.

38
00:02:46.763 --> 00:02:48.543
No single pond has to last.

39
00:02:49.863 --> 00:02:52.583
Refounding balances loss at a level above zero.

40
00:02:53.733 --> 00:02:55.853
The model is deliberately simple.

41
00:02:57.133 --> 00:03:01.693
Its ponds are identical, all equally reachable, with rates that never change.

42
00:03:03.233 --> 00:03:06.233
Real networks have big and small, near and far ponds.

43
00:03:07.613 --> 00:03:12.353
And a small network can lose every pond by chance, even when colonization wins.
